@brief
A ticket, not an exercise.

    ORD-530  Finish the custId rename, make the resolver spec async,
             and flag the 5xx errors for Sentry.

Five files are open. `:bn` moves through them in order; `:wqa` at
the end.

  1. order.provider.ts — `custId` becomes `customerId`. Every one of
     them; the entity already uses `customerId`.

  2. order.resolver.ts — the same rename, except that the GraphQL
     argument is still called `custId` and the schema is not changing
     in this ticket. So `args: { custId: string }` and `args.custId`
     stay. The local variables and the calls into the provider change.

  3. order.resolver.spec.ts — four tests still call the resolver
     synchronously. `async` on the `it(` line, `await` after the `=`
     on the line below. The `custId` inside the test arguments is the
     GraphQL argument and stays.

  4. errors.ts — every entry whose status code is 5xx gets
     `sentry: true`. The 4xx entries keep `sentry: false`.

  5. order.entity.ts — reference only. Leave it alone.

Each file wants a different tool. You have four: a substitute, `cgn`
with `.`, a macro, and `:g`. Each has one place where it is the
obvious choice and three where it is a struggle.

@keys
| `:%s/old/new/g` | every occurrence, no questions asked |
| `cgn` then `.` and `n` | change this match, change the next, skip the next |
| `:%s/old/new/gc` | the same job, asking each time |
| `qa` … `q`, `99@a` | a macro, until it fails |
| `:g/pattern/s/old/new/` | a substitute on only the lines that match |

@hints
When every occurrence changes the same way, do not think. `:%s` is the
tool and anything else is slower.
--
When most occurrences change and a few do not, you need to see each
one. `cgn` and `.` give you that, with `n` to skip. `:%s///gc` is the
same idea from the command line.
--
When the edit touches two lines and has an `Esc` in the middle, it is
a macro. When it is one line and can be named with a pattern, `:g`
with a substitute or `:g` with `normal` is less to type than a
recording.
--
Provider: `:%s/custId/customerId/g`. Resolver: `/custId`, `n`, then
`cgncustomerId` `Esc`, then `nn.` three times. Spec: the search
macro from lesson 7. Errors: `:g/statusCode: 5/s/false/true/`.

@solution
A route, file by file.

**order.provider.ts** — `:%s/custId/customerId/g`. Seven occurrences,
all of them change, nothing to look at. Twenty-five keystrokes.

**order.resolver.ts** — `/custId` lands on the argument type, which
stays. `n` to the local variable, `cgncustomerId` and `Esc`. From
there each pair is one to skip and one to change: `nn.` three times.
`:%s/custId/customerId/gc` with `n y n y n y n y` costs the same and
is fine too. The wrong tool here is the bare `:%s`, which changes the
argument name and breaks the API.

**order.resolver.spec.ts** — `qa/it(.*, () =><Enter>f(;iasync <Esc>j0f=wiawait <Esc>q`
then `99@a`. Two lines per test with an `Esc` in between is the shape
a substitute cannot do and `:g//normal` can only do with `Ctrl-V Esc`
on the command line.

**errors.ts** — `:g/statusCode: 5/s/false/true/`. One line per
target, a pattern that names the line, a one-word change on it. A
macro would work and would take twice as many keys to record.

**order.entity.ts** — nothing. `:wqa`.

About a hundred and fifty keystrokes.

The judgement, stated once so you can carry it out of this chapter:

- Every occurrence, same change: `:s`.
- Most occurrences, a few exceptions you must see: `cgn` and `.`, or
  `:s` with `c`.
- Whole lines you can name with a pattern, one change each: `:g`
  with `s` or with `normal`.
- Anything with an `Esc` in the middle, anything that spans lines,
  anything you would rather do once by hand and not think about
  again: a macro. Recursive if it has a guard, counted if it does not,
  and driven by a search when the targets are scattered.

None of these is better. The skill is recognising the shape of the
edit before your hands start.

@start order.provider.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(protected orderRepository: OrderRepository) {}

  public async findByCustomer(custId: string) {
    const orders = await this.orderRepository.findMany({ where: { custId } });

    if (!orders.length) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND', { custId });
    }

    return orders;
  }

  public async countForCustomer(custId: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.count({ where: { custId } });
  }

  public async latestForCustomer(custId: string) {
    const [latest] = await this.orderRepository.findMany({
      where: { custId },
      orderBy: { placedAt: 'desc' },
      take: 1,
    });

    return latest;
  }
}

@expected order.provider.ts
import { exception } from '../../configuration/errors';
import { OrderRepository } from './order.repository';

export class OrderProvider {
  constructor(protected orderRepository: OrderRepository) {}

  public async findByCustomer(customerId: string) {
    const orders = await this.orderRepository.findMany({ where: { customerId } });

    if (!orders.length) {
      throw exception('ORDER_NOT_FOUND', { customerId });
    }

    return orders;
  }

  public async countForCustomer(customerId: string) {
    return this.orderRepository.count({ where: { customerId } });
  }

  public async latestForCustomer(customerId: string) {
    const [latest] = await this.orderRepository.findMany({
      where: { customerId },
      orderBy: { placedAt: 'desc' },
      take: 1,
    });

    return latest;
  }
}

@start order.resolver.ts
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async orders(args: { custId: string }) {
    const custId = args.custId;

    return this.orderProvider.findByCustomer(custId);
  }

  public async orderCount(args: { custId: string }) {
    const custId = args.custId;

    return this.orderProvider.countForCustomer(custId);
  }
}

@expected order.resolver.ts
import { OrderProvider } from './order.provider';

export class OrderResolver {
  constructor(protected orderProvider: OrderProvider) {}

  public async orders(args: { custId: string }) {
    const customerId = args.custId;

    return this.orderProvider.findByCustomer(customerId);
  }

  public async orderCount(args: { custId: string }) {
    const customerId = args.custId;

    return this.orderProvider.countForCustomer(customerId);
  }
}

@start order.resolver.spec.ts
import { OrderResolver } from './order.resolver';

describe('OrderResolver', () => {
  it('lists orders for a customer', () => {
    const result = resolver.orders({ custId: 'cus_7' });
    expect(result).toHaveLength(3);
  });

  it('returns an empty list for an unknown customer', () => {
    const result = resolver.orders({ custId: 'cus_999' });
    expect(result).toEqual([]);
  });

  it('counts orders for a customer', () => {
    const count = resolver.orderCount({ custId: 'cus_7' });
    expect(count).toBe(3);
  });

  it('counts zero for an unknown customer', () => {
    const count = resolver.orderCount({ custId: 'cus_999' });
    expect(count).toBe(0);
  });
});

@expected order.resolver.spec.ts
import { OrderResolver } from './order.resolver';

describe('OrderResolver', () => {
  it('lists orders for a customer', async () => {
    const result = await resolver.orders({ custId: 'cus_7' });
    expect(result).toHaveLength(3);
  });

  it('returns an empty list for an unknown customer', async () => {
    const result = await resolver.orders({ custId: 'cus_999' });
    expect(result).toEqual([]);
  });

  it('counts orders for a customer', async () => {
    const count = await resolver.orderCount({ custId: 'cus_7' });
    expect(count).toBe(3);
  });

  it('counts zero for an unknown customer', async () => {
    const count = await resolver.orderCount({ custId: 'cus_999' });
    expect(count).toBe(0);
  });
});

